From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 19:37:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] libfetch: follow 307 Temporary Redirect

The status-line switch in http_request() handles 301, 302 and 303 by
falling through to header parsing so that the Location header is read,
but it has no case for 307.  A 307 reply therefore lands in the default
arm, which calls http_seterr() and, unless the 'v' flag is set, leaves
via ouch immediately -- so the redirect is never followed and the
request fails.

The rest of the machinery is already prepared for it:
HTTP_TEMP_REDIRECT is one of the codes HTTP_REDIRECT() matches, which
is what gates both the hdr_location case that builds the new URL and
the retry at the bottom of the loop.

Signed-off-by: Tobias Koch <tobias.koch@aeltra.eu>
---
 libfetch/http.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/libfetch/http.c b/libfetch/http.c
index 0f9fc7c..bac6478 100644
--- a/libfetch/http.c
+++ b/libfetch/http.c
@@ -952,6 +952,7 @@ http_request(struct url *URL, const char *op, struct url_stat *us,
 		case HTTP_MOVED_PERM:
 		case HTTP_MOVED_TEMP:
 		case HTTP_SEE_OTHER:
+		case HTTP_TEMP_REDIRECT:
 			/*
 			 * Not so fine, but we still have to read the
 			 * headers to get the new location.
